---
/* =========================================================================
   BACK TO TOP — VARIANT C · "TOC-integrated Top link"   (PARKED, not active)

   No floating chrome at all. A single "↑ Top" entry pinned at the head of the
   sticky sidebar outline, set in the outline's own register, above the
   "Contents" label. It appears only where the sidebar appears — i.e. at
   ≥70rem, on post pages with an outline and on /research/ — so narrow
   viewports get nothing whatsoever. The most minimal of the four, and the only
   one that adds no new layer to the page.

   ACTIVATE — one line in `src/components/TableOfContents.astro`, as the first
   child of the sidebar branch's `<nav class={classes}>` (i.e. immediately
   above `<span class="toc__label">`):

       <BackToTopC />

   (plus the import in that file's frontmatter:
       import BackToTopC from './back-to-top/BackToTopC.astro';)

   The include site is `TableOfContents.astro` rather than `Base.astro` on
   purpose: this variant *is* an outline entry, and the outline is the only
   place it can live. Putting it in the sidebar branch only (not the `mobile`
   `<details>` branch) is also deliberate — a "Top" link inside a collapsed
   accordion you have to open first is worse than no link.

   REVEAL: CSS scroll-driven animation — `animation-timeline: scroll(root
   block)`, `animation-range: 70vh 100vh`, `both` fill. Same curve as the other
   variants, so a pairing (see the README) reveals both halves together.
   Zero JavaScript.

   FALLBACK (no `animation-timeline: scroll()`): always visible. Harmless
   here — it is a nav link inside a nav block, and at scroll 0 it simply points
   at where you already are. No script: 0 bytes of JS in every browser.

   SCROLL TARGET: `href="#"` (document top), not `#top`. The sidebar outline is
   `position: sticky`, which makes it a containing block for absolutely
   positioned descendants — so a `<span id="top">` emitted from inside it
   cannot be pinned to the top of the page the way it is in variants A, B and
   D. `#` resolves to the top of the document directly and needs no anchor
   element at all, which suits the variant that is trying to add the least.
   `html { scroll-behavior: smooth }` animates it and already yields to
   `prefers-reduced-motion`.
   ========================================================================= */
---

<a class="btt-c" href="#">
  <span class="btt-c__arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8593;</span>
  <span>Top</span>
</a>

<style>
  /* The outline's entries are right-aligned (they hug the article's left edge),
     so this sits on the same right edge, above the label, separated by nothing
     more than space — a hairline here would compete with the label's own rule
     on the research outline. */
  .btt-c {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
    font-family: var(--font-heading-stack);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 120ms ease;
  }

  .btt-c:hover,
  .btt-c:focus-visible {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .btt-c__arrow {
    /* The heading face has no arrow; borrow the body face's and drop the
       tracking so the glyph does not float away from its word. */
    font-family: var(--font-body-stack);
    letter-spacing: 0;
    margin-inline-end: 0.15em;
  }

  /* ---- reveal: scroll-driven, zero JS ---------------------------------- */
  /* Longhands, never the `animation` shorthand: the CSS minifier folds
     `animation-timeline` into the shorthand, which is not a valid component of
     it, and Chromium then drops the whole declaration — a reveal that silently
     never reveals. Keeping `animation-name` separate leaves nothing to fold. */
  @supports (animation-timeline: scroll()) {
    .btt-c {
      animation-name: btt-c-in;
      /* Required for a scroll-driven animation: the timeline is the duration. */
      animation-duration: auto;
      animation-timing-function: linear;
      animation-fill-mode: both;
      animation-timeline: scroll(root block);
      animation-range: 70vh 100vh;
    }
  }

  /* `visibility` rather than `display`: the entry keeps its box, so revealing
     it never shifts the outline's entries down mid-scroll. */
  @keyframes btt-c-in {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      visibility: hidden;
    }
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      visibility: visible;
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .btt-c {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>
